rt in the last week of  january but on january 28th it was  revealed that sue's report would only be  11 pages long and that key details would  be left out in order to quote unquote  avoid any prejudice to their  investigation on top of this johnson got  to review the contents of the reports  for three days before it was finally  published on january 31st 2022  unfortunately for john's senate some of  the worst public and political backlash  we've seen in modern history would  follow soon after  on the surface it seems like people are  pissed off that johnson didn't take kobe  that seriously when it comes to his  personal actions but i think people's  distaste for johnson is much more deep  rooted than him simply not taking covert  seriously a bunch of world leaders did  not take the pandemic that seriously but  they're not receiving the same backlash  that johnson is and i think the key  reason for this is that they were  consistent unlike johnson take trump for  example he didn't take the pandemic too  seriously he often held a massive  gatherings at the white house and he  even held a rally with thousands of  people in june of 2020 while trump did  receive a lot of criticism from the  media and the democrats republicans  didn't exactly turn on him because he  was consistent with his messaging  trump's entire premise was that the  illness was here to stay and that  everyone should return to normal life  including returning to work and school  whether you agree with this perspective  or not is besides the point the point is  that trump practiced what he preached  johnson however did not to please the  media and the part of the public johnson  implemented severe covered restrictions  but in his personal life he didn't  follow any of these this left quite a  sour taste in many british people's  mouths as it felt like johnson and his  colleagues thought that they were above  the law while everyday citizens were  limited from going to work attending  school and opening up the local  businesses johnson was behaving like  nothing was happening what's even more  surprising though than the public being  upset is that politicians are also upset  all the comments on the initial video i  showed you guys are gushing over how  crazy it is to finally see a politician  being held accountable and while i  agreed that it's nice that johnson's  peers are calling him out on his actions  i also think that many of them unlikely  have ulterior motives here's the thing  prime ministers aren't directly voted  for in the uk  rather british citizens vote for members  of parliament and the leader of the  majority party becomes a prime minister  as a result it's the local elections  that determine the national leader this  might be a bit confusing because it's  basically the opposite in the u.s  americans mainly just vote for a  president and all of their local  election choices are just the candidates  from the same party as a result it's an  american politician's best interest to  align with the winning candidate in the  uk though given that citizens don't vote  for the prime minister directly local  candidates don't really care about  aligning with the prime minister  in fact it would probably be beneficial  for them if boris johnson is ousted  because it gives them a chance to move  up with that being said johnson's  colleagues turning on him may have more  to do with politics than serving justice  but hey at least he's being held  accountable  at the end of the day johnson seemingly  elitist viewer along with his colleagues  lack of dependence on him have really  torn apart johnson's reputation  but johnson has had one saving grace  which is russia's invasion of ukraine  russia's invasion has turned  international attention away from  johnson and on to putin zelinski russia  ukraine nato and so on and so forth in  other words the severity of putin's  actions has made most people forget  about johnson's actions aside from  diverting attention russia's invasion  has given johnson the perfect  opportunity to redeem himself by  supporting ukraine letting in refugees  and acting as a western hero in the war  refugees countries had a  historic and proud role in  in taking refugees all of his efforts  seemed to be paying off as the distaste  for johnson has noticeably declined over  the past two months from 73 down to 63  johnson still has a lot of work to do to  win back support from his colleagues and  the public but given that uk's next  general election isn't taking place till  may 2nd 2024 johnson has plenty of time  to do damage control  also in terms of resignation given that  johnson hasn't already resigned it's  extremely unlikely that johnson will  resign due to partygate and johnson will  likely just finish out his term but  that's just what i think  do you guys think johnson should be  fired comment that down below  also drop a like if you think all  politicians need to be held more  accountable and of course consider  checking out our international channels  to watch our videos in other languages  and consider subscribing to see more  questions logically answered but until  then i'm ari and i'll see you guys on  the next one  [Music]
 

